-- title:
--   Theorem 19: partial-isometry mixing of Kraus representations
-- statement:
--   All Hilbert spaces have positive finite dimension. Channels are completely positive and trace preserving; conjugates use the same normalized pure-environment dilation. If $G_k$ is a minimal normalized Kraus family of size $\kappa$, then every rectangular $U$ with $U^\dagger U=I_\kappa$ gives normalized $F_j=\sum_k u_{jk}G_k$ representing the same channel. More generally, for any normalized families $F,G$, let $\kappa$ be the minimum Kraus number of the channel generated by $G$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \Phi_F=\Phi_G\iff\exists W:\ WW^\dagger W=W,\quad\operatorname{rank}W=\kappa,\quad F_j=\sum_k w_{jk}G_k.
--   $$
--
--   The witness can also satisfy $G_k=\sum_j\overline{w_{jk}}F_j$, as supplied by the source proof. No equality of family sizes is required.
-- source:
--   Properties of Conjugate Channels with Applications to Additivity and Multiplicativity, arXiv:quant-ph/0509126v3, PDF p. 30, Theorem 19: partial-isometry mixing of Kraus representations

